.TH KINTSUGI-RPC 1 "2026-10-22" "kintsugi 0.8.0" "Kintsugi Manual"
.SH NAME
kintsugi-rpc \- JSON-RPC loopback API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kintsugi rpc
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR stdio|tcp|http ]
.RB [ \-\-addr
.IR host:port ]
.RB [ \-\-auth
.IR secret ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Serve the harness over JSON-RPC 2.0 so an external client \- an editor, a web UI,
another agent \- can drive it.
.PP
The server binds loopback only. \fBstdio\fR speaks newline-delimited JSON on
standard input and output. \fBtcp\fR speaks one JSON-RPC message per line, with an
optional shared secret sent as a leading \fBAUTH <secret>\fR line. \fBhttp\fR
accepts a POST per request, with the secret sent as
\fBAuthorization: Bearer <secret>\fR.
.PP
A non-loopback address is refused rather than bound: the API carries the
operator's tool authority, so it is never reachable from the network.
.SH METHODS
.TP
.B initialize
Report the server's protocol version and capabilities.
.TP
.B session/new
Open a session; returns its id.
.TP
.B session/prompt
Run one turn in a session.
.TP
.B session/cancel
Abandon the turn in flight.
.TP
.B session/list
List the sessions this server knows.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP \fB\-\-mode\fR
Transport: stdio (default), tcp, or http.
.IP \fB\-\-addr\fR
Listen address for tcp/http. Must be loopback. Default 127.0.0.1:9876.
.IP \fB\-\-auth\fR
Shared secret required by tcp/http clients.
.SH EXAMPLES
Speak JSON-RPC on stdio:
.PP
  $ kintsugi rpc
.PP
Serve on loopback with a secret:
.PP
  $ kintsugi rpc --mode tcp --addr 127.0.0.1:9876 --auth "$FORGE_RPC_SECRET"
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kintsugi (1),
.BR kintsugi-run (1)
